    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The capacitor is physically resisting the current because electrons literally cannot cross the dielectric gap between the plates; they must instead accumulate or leave the plates to create the changing voltage that drives the AC current. This physical accumulation process means that for every voltage change, you must first delay some time to move enough charge to cause that change, which physically constrains the rate of flow. So while the resistance of a resistor heats up from collisions, the capacitor \"resists\" by storing energy temporarily, effectively slowing down how quickly the signal can travel through the component compared to a simple conductor.",
